        { "$comment": "P69 round 4 (lane cs): THE NAMES THAT SELECT A CALLING CONVENTION — this row and the six below. `conventions` lists the ids the name can select, one per target family that has it, each as a TARGET document spells it (a row of its `callingConventions`, or an entry of its `unimplementedCallingConventions`); the answer is read from the pair being compiled for, never from an architecture name. The pair's ACTIVE convention: accepted, no effect, silent — the attribute states what every call already is. ANOTHER convention that target's toolchains emit: REFUSED BY NAME — a function of another convention would be called, and would receive its arguments, by the wrong sequence. NO convention of that target: the name is a word its toolchains do not have, and it is warned as an unknown attribute, as they warn. MEASURED before these rows: `__attribute__((ms_abi))` on an ELF function was warned as an unknown linkage specifier and the function was called with the System V sequence. MEASURED on the references (lane cs's probes ta6 and ta8: a function defined with the attribute, called, and its type compared with the plain one) — `ms_abi`: a distinct convention on x86_64 Linux (gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3), the active one on mingw-w64 gcc (silent), a distinct one on Apple clang and on clang for aarch64 Linux, unknown to arm64 Linux gcc.", "names": ["ms_abi"], "effect": "callingConvention", "conventions": ["ms_x64_", "ms_arm64"] },
